534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440389 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440380 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440389 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440389 1077 "field-1" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440389 1078 #edb/ref 17592186440380 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440389 1079 #uuid "8fcd58ca-53e4-51a1-8c9a-e0b485e4a754" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440390 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440381 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440390 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440390 1077 "field-1" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440390 1078 #edb/ref 17592186440381 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440390 1079 #uuid "b7069eb3-7077-523c-883d-c4e8428dace8" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440391 1075 #edb/ref 17592186440382 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440391 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440391 1077 "field-1" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440391 1078 #edb/ref 17592186440382 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440391 1079 #uuid "d9ab1077-8f29-5a4e-a28a-f16902739e34" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440386 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440392 1069 "q-134231" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440392 1071 "Matt and Andrea each bought $6$ ounces of salted peanuts and $4$ ounces of unsalted peanuts. In total, they bought$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$ounces of peanuts." 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440392 1072 #uuid "02df687b-3c38-56ea-bcbb-7b6992abb8b8" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440392 1074 "We must write and evaluate an expression representing the total amount of peanuts.\n\nSince each of them bought $6$ ounces of salted peanuts and $4$ ounces of unsalted peanuts, the amount of peanuts they purchased, in ounces, is:\n\n$$\n{{6}_{⏟}}_{\\text{salted peanuts}}+{{4}_{⏟}}_{\\text{unsalted peanuts}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* person purchased this amount of peanuts, and there are $2$ people, we must multiply the amount of peanuts by $2.$ Therefore, the total amount of peanuts is:\n\n$$\n{{(6+4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{each person}}\\times {{2}_{⏟}}_{\\text{people}}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operations inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6+4)\\times 2 & =(6+4)\\times 2 \\\\  & =10\\times 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply:\n\n$$\n10\\times 2=20\n$$" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440385 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440393 1069 "q-134235" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440393 1071 "Of the $3$ pounds of walnuts that Robert bought, he gave $\\frac{3}{5}$ pounds to his granddaughter Sophia. As a mixed number, Robert has{{field-1}}\npounds of walnuts remaining." 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440393 1072 #uuid "357eb628-a446-5e21-9526-dabfe7335132" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440393 1074 "To find out how many walnuts Robert had remaining, we must calculate the difference $3-\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nTo subtract $\\frac{3}{5}$ from $3,$ we need to express the whole number $3$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $5.$\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $5$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 5}=\\frac{15}{5}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{15-3}{5}=\\frac{12}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n12÷5=2\\,\\text{R}\\,2=2\\,\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Robert had $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}$ pounds of walnuts remaining." 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440390 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440394 1069 "q-134237" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440394 1071 "Susan ate $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the walnuts that she bought. What **exact** amount of walnuts did she eat?\n\n*Remember that Susan bought $\\frac{17}{9}$ of a pound of walnuts.*\n\nSusan ate$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$pounds of walnuts." 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440394 1072 #uuid "56c0d0ac-e441-53cb-9b72-c25279d28ac0" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440394 1074 "We know that Susan has $\\frac{17}{9}$ of a pound of walnuts.\n\nTo calculate the amount that she ate, we need to multiply $\\frac{1}{3}$ by $\\frac{17}{9}.$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{17}{9}=\\frac{1\\times 17}{3\\times 9}=\\frac{17}{27}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Susan ate $\\frac{17}{27}$ of  a pound of walnuts." 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440388 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440389 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440395 1069 "q-134236" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440395 1071 "After Sophia takes her share of walnuts, Robert's remaining amount of walnuts is{{field-1}}\nthan Susan's amount of walnuts because $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}$\n{{field-2}} $\\frac{17}{9}.$" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440395 1072 #uuid "994437db-5e6c-52cd-85e4-193a53ab9c7f" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440395 1074 "Robert has $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}$ pounds of walnuts remaining, and Susan has $\\frac{17}{9}$ pounds of walnuts.\n\nWe cannot compare the fractions directly as they don't have a common denominator.\n\nWe can make a common denominator of $5\\times 9=45.$\n\nTo put $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}$ over a denominator of $45,$ we first convert to an improper fraction and then multiply the numerator and denominator by $9:$\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{12}{5}=\\frac{12\\times 9}{5\\times 9}=\\frac{108}{45}\n$$\n\nTo put $\\frac{17}{9}$ over a denominator of $45,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{17}{9}=\\frac{17\\times 5}{9\\times 5}=\\frac{85}{45}\n$$\n\nWe can now compare the fractions $\\frac{108}{45}$ and $\\frac{85}{45}$ by looking at the numerators only.\n\nSince $108>85,$ the correct statement must be:\n\n$$\n\\frac{108}{45}>\\frac{85}{45}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is\n\n> \"After Sophia takes her walnuts, Robert's remaining amount of walnuts is $>$ than Susan's amount of walnuts because $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}>\\frac{17}{9}.$\"" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440391 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440396 1069 "q-134232" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440396 1071 "Carla has $12$ packages of almonds, each weighing $42$ ounces. In total, Carla has$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$ounces of almonds." 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440396 1072 #uuid "b88dbf04-dd80-54e8-8fde-e4783f40c410" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440396 1074 "To calculate the total amount of almonds, we need to multiply $12$ by $42.$\n\nSo, we write it this way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}12\\times 2=24\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}12\\times 40=480\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}24+480=504\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12\\times 42=504.$\n\nTherefore, Carla has $504$ ounces of almonds in total." 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440387 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440397 1069 "q-134234" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440397 1071 "Robert bought $3$ pounds of walnuts while Susan bought $\\frac{17}{9}$ of a pound of walnuts. How many walnuts did they buy together in total? Express your final answer as a mixed number.\n\nThey bought$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$pounds of walnuts in total." 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440397 1072 #uuid "c1e68ffc-d3ca-5167-817a-cc856c132a73" 13194139534835 17592186045417 true] [17592186440397 1074 "To determine the number of walnuts Robert and Susan bought together in total, we have to calculate the following sum:\n\n$$\n3+\\frac{17}{9}\n$$\n\nTo add $3$ and $\\frac{17}{9},$ we need to express the whole number $3$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $9.$\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThen we put this fraction over a denominator of $9$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $9:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 9}{1\\times 9}=\\frac{27}{9}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{9}+\\frac{17}{9}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{9}+\\frac{17}{9}=\\frac{27+17}{9}=\\frac{44}{9}\n$$\n\nExpressing our answer as a mixed number, we have\n\n$$\n44÷9=4\\,\\text{R}\\,8=4\\,\\frac{8}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, they bought $4\\,\\frac{8}{9}$ pounds of walnuts." 13194139534835 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-186dfbec-40e1-5669-b78b-1a6e666b138c" 17592186440378 "answer-48840b8b-45fc-5af4-9eaf-3b0